
  <1>riscvsim<0> <36>›<0> <1>demo 2<0> · Waiting for memory
  <90>See why an add must wait one cycle for a load.<0>

  <90> 1  # A load reads memory in MEM, one stage later than the<0>
  <90> 2  # ALU, so the add right after it must wait one cycle.<0>
   3  .data
   4  value:  .word 41
   5
   6  .text
   7  addi x5, x0, 1     # x5 = 1
  <90> 8  # lui puts 0x10000 in the upper 20 bits of x6, so x6<0>
  <90> 9  # shows 0x10000000: the address where .data starts.<0>
  10  lui  x6, 0x10000   # x6 = 0x10000000, where value is
  11  lw   x7, 0(x6)     # x7 = 41, read from memory
  12  add  x10, x7, x5   # needs x7 now: one stall

  <1>cycle 1<0>
    <36>IF<0> <1>addi x5, x0, 1<0>  <36>ID<0> <90>·<0>  <36>EX<0> <90>·<0>  <36>MEM<0> <90>·<0>  <36>WB<0> <90>·<0>
    <36>›<0> The first instruction, addi, is fetched from memory.

  <1>cycle 2<0>
    <36>IF<0> <1>lui x6, 0x10000<0>  <36>ID<0> <1>addi x5, x0, 1<0>  <36>EX<0> <90>·<0>  <36>MEM<0> <90>·<0>  <36>WB<0> <90>·<0>
    <36>›<0> addi is decoded and reads its source registers.

  <1>cycle 3<0>
    <36>IF<0> <1>lw x7, 0(x6)<0>  <36>ID<0> <1>lui x6, 0x10000<0>  <36>EX<0> <1>addi x5, x0, 1<0>  <36>MEM<0> <90>·<0>  <36>WB<0> <90>·<0>
    <36>›<0> addi is computing in the ALU; everything else moves one stage on.

  <1>cycle 4<0>
    <36>IF<0> <1>add x10,x7,x5<0>  <36>ID<0> <1>lw x7, 0(x6)<0>  <36>EX<0> <1>lui x6…<0>  <36>MEM<0> <1>addi x5,x0,1<0>  <36>WB<0> <90>·<0>
    <36>›<0> lui is computing in the ALU; everything else moves one stage on.

  <1>cycle 5<0>
    <36>IF<0> <90>·<0>  <36>ID<0> <1>add x10,x7,x5<0>  <36>EX<0> <1>lw x7, 0(x6)<0>  <36>MEM<0> <1>lui x6…<0>  <36>WB<0> <1>addi x5,x0,1<0>
    <32>✓<0> <1;32>x5<0> = <1;32>1<0>
    <33>!<0> lw is reading memory. add must wait one cycle because it needs x7 from
      lw.
      <90>A stall: the instruction stays where it is for a cycle, and an empty<0>
      <90>slot (a bubble) moves on instead.<0>

  <1>cycle 6<0>
    <36>IF<0> <90>·<0>  <36>ID<0> <1>add x10, x7, x5<0>  <36>EX<0> <33>bubble<0>  <36>MEM<0> <1>lw x7, 0(x6)<0>  <36>WB<0> <1>lui x6, 0x10000<0>
    <32>✓<0> <1;32>x6<0> = <1;32>0x10000000<0>
    <36>›<0> lui finishes and writes x6 = 0x10000000.

  <1>cycle 7<0>
    <36>IF<0> <90>·<0>  <36>ID<0> <90>·<0>  <36>EX<0> <1>add x10, x7, x5<0>  <36>MEM<0> <33>bubble<0>  <36>WB<0> <1>lw x7, 0(x6)<0>
    <32>✓<0> <1;32>x7<0> = <1;32>41<0>
    <36>›<0> add needs x7 from lw, so it is forwarded from the MEM/WB pipeline
      register and nobody waits.
      <90>Forwarding: a result goes straight to the instruction that needs it,<0>
      <90>before it is written to its register.<0>
      <90>A pipeline register holds an instruction and its values between two<0>
      <90>stages.<0>

  <1>cycle 8<0>
    <36>IF<0> <90>·<0>  <36>ID<0> <90>·<0>  <36>EX<0> <90>·<0>  <36>MEM<0> <1>add x10, x7, x5<0>  <36>WB<0> <33>bubble<0>
    <36>›<0> Nothing is left to fetch, so the pipeline is emptying.

